  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Data to the Display\r\n\r\nYour task is to use the data **schema** from the previous level to populate and display data for three food names on the screen.\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Sample Schema\r\n- foodNames: string(\"peanut butter\", \"pizza\", etc.)\r\n- allergen_name: string (\"peanuts\",\"gluten\", etc.)\r\n- severity: integer (1-mild, 2-moderate, etc.)\r\n- symptoms: string (\"rash\", \"swollen tongue\", etc.)\r\n- treatment: string (\"epiPen\", \"anti-histamine\", etc)\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Step 1: \r\n\r\n **Update HTML and CSS:** Using the schema above, or modify as you see fit, and work with AI to update the `index.html` and `style.css` files. Ensure they contain the structure and styling for the fields you want to display based on your schema.\r\n\r\n*Sample prompt: Update `index.html` and `style.css` so it has a clear layout to display three food items, and for each item, show all four fields from the schema (allergen_name, severity, symptoms, treatment). Use semantic HTML (like sections, headings, and paragraphs or list items).*\r\n\r\nCopy and paste the new code to replace the existing code. Tweak as necessary.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: \r\n\r\n **Define Parallel Arrays (Data):** In your `script.js` file, define three or more **parallel arrays** (lists) that hold your food data.\r\n \r\n - Each array must have at least three items.\r\n- **Critical:** The item at the same **index** (e.g., index `[0]`) across all arrays must belong to the food allergen category.\r\n\r\n*Example:* `const allergen_names = [\"peanut\", \"gluten\", \"shellfish\"];`\r\n    \r\n## Step 3: \r\n\r\n3.  **Manually Wire the Data (JavaScript):** Complete the empty `updateDisplay` function in `script.js`. Write code that uses **manual indexing** to assign array values to the correct HTML element.\r\n\r\n    * **Card 1:** Use the DOM variables for Card 1 (e.g., `name1`) and access the data at array **index `[0]`** (e.g., `foodNames[0]`).\r\n    * **Card 2:** Use the DOM variables for Card 2 (e.g., `name2`) and access the data at array **index `[1]`**.\r\n    * **Card 3:** Use the DOM variables for Card 3 (e.g., `name3`) and access the data at array **index `[2]`**.\r\n    \r\n*Example:*   \r\n  // Card 1\r\n  \r\n  `name1.textContent = foodNames[0];`\r\n\r\n  `allergen1.textContent = allergenNames[0];`\r\n  \r\n## Step 4:\r\n\r\n **Test:** Run your code. The three food cards in the preview should now correctly display the specific food name, allergen status, and safety note you defined in your arrays.\r\n\r\n",
    "submittable": "false",
    "hide_share_and_remix": "false",
